From: MOSS Windows lane <moss@example.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The delete-child deny on a denied target's parent, on the parent alone

---

Upstream: anthropics/sandbox-runtime, tag v0.0.81 (c40cf14), after 0001-0003.
Reason: every deny srt-win stamps also puts a FILE_DELETE_CHILD deny on the
target's parent, inheritable (OI|CI). Writing an inheritable entry makes the
system walk everything below the object to bring its inherited entries up to
date, so a target at the top of a profile walks the whole profile, at stamp
and again at restore: measured on windows-latest, one deny on a new folder at
the top of a 50,829-entry profile took 21-27 s, one a level down 0.2-0.4 s,
with or without the profile read grant. The delete-child check that protects
the target is made on its parent object alone, so the parent's entry need not
be handed down; on the parent alone it changes nothing objects below inherit,
and srt-win's own writer (`commit_dacl`, `same_inheritable`) writes the
parent's list on the object without the walk.

What the inherited entry also did: it kept FILE_DELETE_CHILD denied on the
target and everything below it. A read deny (FILE_ALL) denies that right
itself; a write deny's mask holds DELETE but not FILE_DELETE_CHILD, and a
delete is granted by the parent's FILE_DELETE_CHILD whatever the child's own
DELETE says. So a write deny now carries its own inheritable delete-child deny
on the target: written with the target's deny, it walks only the target's own
tree, which that deny walks already.

Files: src/acl.rs; tests/persistent_grant_access_check.rs (one access-check case).

What changes, and nothing else, per session (`SbAceSet::aces`) and persistent
(`persistent_aces`): the parent's FILE_DELETE_CHILD deny is written without
OI|CI; a write-denied target gains `(D;OICI;FILE_DELETE_CHILD)`. Three unit tests
of the persistent shape follow (one on disk), and an access check of both entries.

